+# Proof outline — `maximalLength_le_isBigO`
+
+The frozen proposition is an existential statement.  Its witness is a
+function `Ω : ℕ → ℝ` satisfying a Big-O relation and a pointwise bound; the
+source type does not require `Ω` to be nonnegative.  The target-local proof
+keeps that literal type and chooses
+
+```text
+f n = (Real.iteratedLog n : ℝ)
+Ω n = -|f n|.
+```
+
+The source utility and extremal definitions are replayed in `Statement.lean`:
+`Real.iteratedLog`, `ltTwo`, `IsIncreasingTwo`, and the supremum defining
+`maximalLength`.  `FrozenStatement` preserves the source's explicit `Ω` and
+`n` binders and its coercions.  `Target` is definitionally identical, with
+both transport directions checked by `Audit.lean`.
+
+For the pointwise clause, fix an admissible list `s` of triples.  If `n ≥ 2`,
+the first two coordinates map every list position into the grid
+`[1,n] × [1,n]`, which has cardinality `n²`.  A list longer than `n²` has two
+positions with equal first and second coordinates.  Orienting those positions
+by their order and using pairwise `ltTwo` contradicts the finite-coordinate
+lemma `not_ltTwo_of_first_two_eq`; hence `s.length ≤ n²`.
+
+The edge cases are explicit.  For `n = 0`, the interval `[1,0]` is empty and
+every admissible list is empty.  For `n = 1`, every coordinate equals one and
+two list entries would violate pairwise `ltTwo`, so the length is at most one,
+which is `1²`.  The empty list supplies a member of the defining set, and
+`csSup_le` closes `maximalLength n ≤ n²` for all `n`.
+
+Finally, `isBigO_refl` together with norm invariance under negation and absolute
+value proves `f =O[atTop] Ω`.  Since `-|f n| ≤ 0`, positivity and the upper bound
+`exp (-|f n|) ≤ 1` allow `le_div_iff₀` to relax the quadratic bound to the exact
+denominator in the frozen statement.  The root is
+`AwesomeTheorems.Stage5.Theorems.S5_CLM_00003493.theorem_complete`.
